# Abstract storage layer: feasibility research

Goal being evaluated: introduce a storage abstraction that lets Blot (a) move
blog folder + generated-asset storage off EBS onto S3, for cost/scalability/
deployment reasons, and (b) enforce per-blog storage quotas — ideally with no
read-path performance penalty, paying any cost at sync/build time instead.

**Scope note:** git-backed sync is explicitly out of scope for this effort.
The storage layer doesn't need to know git exists; if/when git sync is
revisited separately, its data can live in the same bucket under a prefix
like `folder/{blogID}/gitClient/...` without the storage layer having any
special knowledge of it.

**Sequencing dependency: don't start building this until `replaceFolderLinks`
is out of the render pipeline.** [PR #1896](https://github.com/davidmerfield/blot/pull/1896)
("Bake CDN links at build time for entries and cache template CSS/JS") is
open and moves folder-link resolution for entries off the per-request render
path, baking resolved CDN URLs in at build time instead of resolving them
live on every request. This is exactly the request-time disk-read risk
flagged in §1.2 (`lookupFile.js`'s live case-insensitive path lookups) —
it needs to be considered solved (that PR merged, and its approach extended
to any render-time call sites it doesn't yet cover) *before* Phase 0 below
starts, not in parallel with it. Building the storage abstraction while
`replaceFolderLinks` is still mid-migration would mean designing around a
moving target for exactly the read path most sensitive to S3's added
latency.

**Verdict up front: feasible, and the codebase is already unusually well-prepared
for it.** The riskiest-sounding pieces — no request-time disk penalty and a
caching layer to soften S3 latency — already exist in some form. The main
net-new work is the abstraction itself, an SSD cache, and quota accounting
(which doesn't exist at all today).

---

## 1. Current architecture (as it stands today)

### 1.1 Two directories, no IO abstraction

`config/index.js:83-89` defines everything storage-related off one env var:

```
data_directory: BLOT_DATA_DIRECTORY
blog_static_files_dir: BLOT_DATA_DIRECTORY + "/static"
blog_folder_dir: BLOT_DATA_DIRECTORY + "/blogs"
```

There is no `app/storage` module today (it's a TODO item, not yet built —
confirmed, no such directory exists). The closest thing to an abstraction is
[`app/helper/localPath.js`](app/helper/localPath.js), which every blog-folder
consumer calls to turn `(blogID, relativePath)` into an absolute path:

```js
module.exports = function (blogID, path) {
  path = pathNormalizer(path);
  const root = join(blog_folder_dir, blogID);
  path = resolve(root, path);   // prevents escaping the blog root
  path = join(root, path);
  return path;
};
```

Critically, `localPath` only resolves a **path string** — it does no IO. Every
caller then does its own raw `fs`/`fs-extra` call against that path. That
means:

- **Blog folder** (`blog_folder_dir`): 15+ files call `localPath(...)` directly
  and then `fs.readFile`/`fs.outputFile`/`fs.remove`/etc themselves —
  `app/models/template/writeToFolder.js`, `removeFromFolder.js`,
  `buildFromFolder.js`, `determineTemplateFolder.js`,
  `app/blog/render/replaceFolderLinks/lookupFile.js`,
  `app/blog/routes/assets.js`, `app/cdn/index.js`, `app/dashboard/site/export.js`,
  `app/models/blog/remove.js`, plus test files and scripts.
- **Static/generated assets** (`blog_static_files_dir`): ~19 files construct
  paths into it directly with `path.join(config.blog_static_files_dir, blogID, ...)`
  — thumbnail creation (`app/build/thumbnail/create.js:21`), every document
  converter (`app/build/converters/{docx,odt,gdoc,img}/index.js`), image
  optimization (`app/build/plugins/image/optimize.js`), link screenshots
  (`app/build/plugins/linkScreenshot/index.js`), dashboard uploads (favicon,
  avatar, template assets), blog removal/export, and the CDN's static file
  route (`app/cdn/index.js:119`, `app/blog/routes/assets.js`). The per-blog
  subdirectories in use are `_thumbnails`, `_image_cache`, `_assets`
  (document-converter output), `_bookmark_screenshots`, `_avatars` and
  `_template_assets` (template editor uploads) — see §2.2.2 for why the last
  two differ from the rest.

So: **no single choke point today**, but also **no exotic IO patterns** — it's
overwhelmingly `fs-extra` calls (`readFile`, `outputFile`, `remove`,
`ensureDir`, `copy`) plus `express.static()` for serving. That's good news: an
abstraction layer is a mechanical (if wide) refactor, not a rewrite of business
logic. This matches almost exactly what your own `TODO` entry proposes
(`app/storage/index.js` exposing `storage.blogs.resolve/readFile`,
`storage.static.writeFile`, etc., wrapping `fs-extra` initially).

**Important for the object-key plan (§4.4):** both `blog_folder_dir` and
`blog_static_files_dir` map to the public CDN URL as a bare `{blogID}/{path}`
today, with no extra prefix. `app/cdn/index.js`'s `/folder/v-:version/...`
route strips the `v-:version` segment (a pure cache-busting token, not part
of any stored path) before resolving the rest of the URL against
`blog_folder_dir` — and its static-assets route mounts `blog_static_files_dir`
at the CDN root, so `/{blogID}/_thumbnails/...` maps directly, no prefix at
all. Neither scope's *stored* path includes a scope-identifying prefix today —
that only gets introduced if a bucket layout adds one (see §2.2).

### 1.2 The read path already avoids per-request disk hits for the expensive stuff

This directly bears on your "no penalty, or pay it at build time" requirement:

- **Rendered output is cached in Redis, not read from disk per request.**
  `app/blog/render/full-view-cache.js` wraps every render in an in-process LRU
  (`lru-cache`, 1000 entries / 20MB, keyed by `blogID + cacheID + templateID +
  viewName`). Underneath that, `app/cdn/index.js:70-113` shows rendered
  template output is fetched by content hash from Redis
  (`client.get(key.renderedOutput(hash))`), with a fallback to a legacy
  disk-backed route (`config.data_directory + "/cdn/template"`, which `TODO`
  already flags for removal). So the single most performance-sensitive read
  path — serving rendered HTML/CSS/JS — has already been moved off cold disk
  reads once, using exactly the "content-addressed, immutable" pattern that's
  ideal for S3 + long-lived CDN caching.
- **Generated assets are built asynchronously, not on request.** Thumbnails
  (`app/build/thumbnail/create.js`), document conversions
  (`app/build/converters/*`), and image optimization all run during
  sync/build — triggered from `app/sync/index.js` → `folder.update()` — and
  write finished files to `blog_static_files_dir` with content-addressed or
  UUID'd paths *before* any visitor requests them. A page load never waits on
  thumbnail generation. This is exactly the "pay the penalty at entry
  build-time" model you want, and it's already how the system works — S3
  upload (and, per §4.4, a Bunny cache-warm ping) would slot into the same
  async step.
- **The known expensive disk operations are folder-link resolution and the
  `folder` template local — both already hit live disk per request, uncached
  or only partially cached.**
  `app/blog/render/replaceFolderLinks/lookupFile.js` does case-insensitive
  path lookups against the live blog folder (with its own 5000-entry
  in-process cache), and `TODO` explicitly flags this ("Resolve bad links...
  at build-time rather than run-time so we can avoid the expensive disk
  lookups"). More significantly, `app/blog/render/retrieve/folder.js` runs a
  live `fs.readdir` + `fs.stat` per file **with no caching at all** every
  time a template uses the `folder` local. These are the two places where
  request-time disk I/O is a known, uncached cost today — worth resolving
  *before* or *alongside* an S3 migration. This gets more important, not
  less, once the blog folder moves to S3: S3 has no real directories, so
  "readdir" becomes a paginated `ListObjectsV2` prefix scan with materially
  different performance characteristics than a local syscall.
- **There's already a second, independent content-cache implementation
  worth consolidating into the same abstraction.**
  `app/helper/transformer/index.js` is a generic content-hash cache used by
  image-cache/optimize and other transforms: it stores result *metadata* in
  Redis but writes/reads the actual files by re-deriving paths into
  `blog_static_files_dir` (and, on fallback, the blog folder) independently
  of `helper/localPath` — i.e. it duplicates path-resolution and
  case-insensitive-fallback logic rather than sharing it. This is a second
  candidate consumer for the new `app/storage` abstraction, and a good
  argument for building the abstraction before adding S3, since two
  independent ad-hoc path-resolution implementations already exist and a
  third (S3 key construction) would only compound the drift.

### 1.3 Blog-folder writes are already serialized per-blog, but the lock is local-disk-based

`app/sync/index.js:64` acquires a `proper-lockfile` lock **on the blog's own
directory on local disk** (`lockPath = localPath(blogID, "/")`) before any
sync writes happen, with a 10s stale timeout and 3s heartbeat. This is what
currently prevents concurrent Dropbox/Google Drive/git syncs from racing on
the same blog folder.

This matters for the "horizontal scalability" goal independently of S3: a
local-filesystem lock only works because sync for a given blog always runs on
one box today. Confirmed via `scripts/deploy/constants.js:44-101`: production
is currently **one EC2 host** running three containers (BLUE/GREEN/YELLOW,
plus a separate AIRLOCK sidecar), all bind-mounting the *same*
`DATA_DIRECTORY_ON_SERVER` (`/var/www/blot/data`) — i.e. one EBS volume
shared by same-host containers, not multiple hosts sharing a bucket yet. So
"multiple node servers interacting with the same S3 bucket" is a real future
goal — the TODO's own framing is that S3 is the *prerequisite* for going
multi-host/multi-region ("Once this is done, the nginx + node server is
'stateless' and could be replicated easily across aws regions"), not a fix
for an existing multi-host bottleneck. But moving to S3 without also moving
this lock to something shared (Redis-based distributed lock — you already run
Redis, e.g. via Redlock) would silently reintroduce race conditions the
moment a second app box exists. **S3 alone doesn't solve concurrency; the
lock has to move too.**

Worth noting separately: production already has an instance-store-backed
path mounted at `/var/instance-ssd` (used today for openresty/nginx logs, per
prior investigation of this box) — so the "huge ephemeral SSD" you want to
use as a cache layer is likely already provisioned on the host and just
needs a data directory carved out on it, not new infrastructure. By contrast,
nothing in `scripts/` or the app today uses instance-store for blog data —
even `tmp_directory` (`config/index.js:85`, used for Pandoc's intermediate
output and downloaded transformer sources) currently sits on the *same*
disk as persistent blog data, so there isn't yet any fast/slow tiering
in the app layer at all, on any disk.

### 1.4 A near-identical SSD-LRU-cache pattern already exists in production code

`app/clients/icloud/macserver/watcher/monitorDiskUsage.js` implements exactly
the pattern you're describing for the S3 caching layer, just for a different
subsystem (the Mac-mini iCloud watcher, which has a fixed local disk budget
for a shared iCloud Drive folder representing many blogs):

- Polls total disk usage via `du -sk` every 15s against a 10GB cap
  (`MAX_DISK_USAGE_BYTES`).
- Tracks last-touched time per blog in an in-memory `Map`.
- Evicts least-recently-updated blogs first when over budget, re-checking
  usage after each eviction.

This is a working, tested precedent for "local SSD as an LRU cache over
a larger backing store" in this exact codebase — worth reusing/generalizing
rather than designing from scratch. The blog-folder SSD cache you're
describing is structurally the same problem (bound local disk, evict
least-recently-used blog, re-fetch from source of truth on next access).

### 1.5 Nothing computes per-blog storage size today, except one offline audit script

Confirmed by searching: there is no per-blog folder size calculation wired
into any request, sync, or enforcement path, and no quota enforcement. This
matches `TODO`'s own list of unimplemented items ("Handle storage quota
errors", "Bug: add a check for folder storage before doing mass upload",
"Bug: handle folder full error appropriately"). The one exception is a manual
audit tool, `scripts/user/identify-overdue-users.js`, which shells out to
`du -sb` against both `blog_static_files_dir/{blogID}` and
`blog_folder_dir/{blogID}` for disabled/unpaid accounts to report reclaimable
space — not automated, not per-blog-quota, but a useful precedent for a
reconciliation script (see the Phase list below). Quotas are otherwise net-new
work, not a refactor — good place to build them directly into the
abstraction layer's write path (increment a per-blog byte counter on every
write through the layer, rather than re-`du`-ing the whole folder), with the
`du`-based script's approach reused periodically to catch counter drift.

### 1.6 No AWS SDK dependency exists yet

`package.json` has no `aws-sdk`/`@aws-sdk/*`. There's no S3 usage anywhere in
`app/`, `config/`, or `scripts/` currently — this is a greenfield integration,
not touching/replacing an existing AWS client. Redis (`redis` 6.1.0,
`ioredis`-free — plain `redis` client) and `fs-extra` are the only
storage-adjacent dependencies in place.

### 1.7 CDN static serving reads straight off local disk today — this is the actual latency risk

`app/cdn/index.js` and `app/blog/routes/assets.js` both use `express.static()`
directly against `blog_folder_dir` / `blog_static_files_dir` /
`GLOBAL_STATIC_FILES`. Bunny sits in front of this as the CDN edge, and
**Bunny's pull zone origin is the Blot app server itself** (confirmed by
`app/cdn/index.js` acting as a full origin-serving router) — not S3 or Bunny
Storage directly. That means the S3 migration itself can be entirely
invisible to Bunny (§4.4), and only the *later*, optional "point Bunny
straight at S3" optimization needs Bunny-side changes.

This is the one place where "no performance penalty" is genuinely at stake:
today, a CDN (Bunny) cache miss means one local disk read on the origin.
Under S3, the same miss means a network round-trip to S3 unless mediated by
the SSD cache layer. The SSD LRU cache in front of these routes (per §1.4)
keeps this invisible for warm assets; only cold/evicted ones touch S3, and
those are rare by definition (Bunny already permacaches most of this traffic
per your own note — confirmed as a real, named Bunny feature, Perma-Cache,
see §4.4).

### 1.8 Prior art / caution: don't reach for Redis as bulk storage again

[Redis hash entry storage rollout] — a related-but-different effort (moving
entry storage into Redis hashes) was fully reverted in PR #1831 after causing
a production performance regression and not solving the problem it targeted
(nashp/anchor slowness). That's not a reason to avoid Redis for *metadata*
(directory listings, file sizes, lock state — small, bounded, already how
rendered-output caching works per §1.2) — but it's a concrete reason to keep
Redis out of the *bulk file content* path, and to benchmark under realistic
concurrent load (you already have `toxiproxy` wired up for exactly this) before
trusting a new Redis-based scheme in production.

---

## 2. Decisions made so far

Captured here as they're made, so this doc stays the single source of truth
rather than scattered across chat.

### 2.1 Abstraction shape

- **Location: `app/storage`, not `app/models/storage`.** `app/models` is
  scoped to *structured domain entities with identity* — records with a
  `scheme.js`/`key.js`/fields, currently Redis-backed but not
  Redis-exclusive by design (SQL is plausible for some models later, e.g.
  for full-text search). A blob-storage layer doesn't have that shape at
  all — it's bytes in/out keyed by path, with no scheme or fields. The
  axis that decides placement is "structured entity vs. opaque blob," not
  "Redis vs. other backing store," and those are orthogonal — so
  broadening what `models` can be backed by doesn't pull storage in with
  it. Practically, `models/blog`, `models/template`, etc. will *depend on*
  the storage layer (the same relationship they already have with
  `models/client` for Redis), not contain it — `app/storage` sits
  alongside `app/models`, `app/helper`, `app/clients`, `app/build` as its
  own top-level concern, matching how the repo already separates
  cross-cutting subsystems.

- **Interface shape: a scoped handle per blog (`storage.for(blogID)`),
  not a class, not flat functions only.** `storage.for(blogID)` returns
  a cheap, stateless object (no connection/lifecycle to manage — nothing
  here is a real resource) exposing one property per named scope, e.g.
  `.folder` and `.assets`:

  ```js
  const store = storage.for(blogID);
  await store.assets.write(path, buffer);
  await store.folder.read(path);
  ```

  A plain factory function rather than `new Storage(...)` — the rest of
  `app/` is written as exported functions/modules, not classes, so a
  constructor would be the only one in the codebase. Flat functions
  (`storage.folder.read(blogID, path)`) can still exist as a thin wrapper
  calling `for` internally, for call sites that only ever do one
  operation.

- **Naming: a deliberately small, custom vocabulary — not `fs-extra`'s
  method names.** `read`/`write`/`delete`/`list`/`exists`/`size`, not
  `readFile`/`outputFile`/`remove`/`readdir`. `fs-extra` supports
  operations that don't map onto S3 at all (symlinks, `chmod`, `watch`,
  atomic rename), and reusing its names would invite callers to assume
  that whole surface is available. A smaller custom vocabulary is also
  easier to `grep`-distinguish from raw `fs` calls during the incremental
  migration, and is a smaller contract to keep behaviorally identical
  across local disk, the SSD cache, and S3.

- **Each named scope (`.folder`, `.assets`, and any added later) is
  configured independently** — its own key prefix, storage class, and
  lifecycle rules are set once per scope in the storage layer's config,
  not passed by callers on every call (this holds even though both scopes
  now live in one bucket — see §2.2). Callers just say *what* and *where
  within their blog*; the layer owns *which prefix/class/lifecycle* a
  given scope maps to.

- **Streaming is a first-class part of the interface, not an afterthought.**
  Confirmed this is workable with S3: uploads of arbitrary size stream via
  `@aws-sdk/lib-storage`'s `Upload` class (handles multipart automatically,
  accepts a Node `Readable` as `Body`, no need to know the total size or
  buffer it all in memory first); downloads stream via
  `GetObjectCommand`'s response, whose `Body` is a Node `Readable` that can
  be piped directly to a response or another destination. So alongside
  whichever buffer-based methods exist (`read`/`write`), the interface
  should also expose stream-shaped equivalents (e.g.
  `store.folder.createReadStream(path)` / `createWriteStream(path)`) backed
  by the same machinery, for large files (folder exports, big media) that
  shouldn't be forced through memory.

- **Quota accounting is generic across scopes, but only enforced for
  `.folder`.** The byte-counter machinery lives in the storage layer for
  both `.folder` and `.assets` (useful for ops/debugging either way), but
  the user-facing quota cap only checks and rejects on `.folder` writes —
  nothing in `.assets` (generated files or dashboard uploads, §2.2.2) is
  counted against the user's limit.

A **cross-cutting requirement that falls out of the above, still needing an
explicit answer**: 64 call sites today branch on `err.code === "ENOENT"`
from raw `fs` calls (confirmed via search). Whatever error type the
interface throws needs one clear, backend-agnostic "not found" signal (e.g.
a `NotFoundError` class) that means the same thing whether the backend is
local disk (`ENOENT`) or S3 (`404`/`NoSuchKey`) — this hasn't been decided
yet, just flagged as necessary.

### 2.2 Bucket topology — revised: one bucket, not two

Earlier in this research a two-bucket split (`.folder` versioned + CRR'd,
`.assets` unversioned) was proposed to resolve a tension between "versioning
off" and "S3 replication needs versioning." That's now superseded by a
simpler framing that changes the risk calculus:

**Key reframe: losing `.folder` data, while bad, is not catastrophic for
most blogs.** Blot's copy of a blog folder is, for most blogs, a replica —
the user's Dropbox/Google Drive/iCloud/git client holds its own copy
independently, so a full resync can recover from most data loss scenarios.
That changes S3-native versioning/replication from "the only thing standing
between you and permanent loss" to "a nice-to-have," and periodic
cross-account backup becomes an acceptable safety net rather than a
compromise.

**The one caveat raised earlier is now resolved, not just noted.**
`app/clients/local` was flagged as a possible exception — a blog with no
external synced copy, where Blot's own copy would be the only one. Confirmed
this doesn't apply to any real, production blog: the local client is
registered only when `config.environment === "development"`
(`app/clients/index.js`), so it's not just discouraged for production use,
it's structurally absent from the client registry in production — there is
no dashboard flow or configuration path by which a real, user-facing blog
could ever end up on it. (Documented explicitly now in
[`app/clients/local/README`](app/clients/local/README), including a note
that changes to this module should preserve that property.) So the "real
users have their own copy" framing holds unconditionally for every
production blog — every client available to a real user (Dropbox, Google
Drive, iCloud, git, SFTP-style clients) syncs from an external source Blot
doesn't own.

**This reframe covers `.folder` only, not `.assets`.** Dashboard uploads —
template editor uploads (`_template_assets`) and avatars (`_avatars`) — are
stored in `.assets` (§2.2.2) and exist *only* in Blot: no sync client holds
a copy, and nothing can regenerate them. So `.assets` can't be treated as
disposable, and the backup below covers it too.

Given that, the decided shape is:

- **One bucket**, with `.folder` under a `folder/{blogID}/{path}` prefix
  and `.assets` at the bucket root as `{blogID}/{path}` — not two buckets.
  `.assets` gets no scope prefix so its keys are identical to its public
  CDN paths (§2.3, §4.4). The two can't collide: blog IDs are always
  `blog_<uuid>`, never `folder`. `.folder` and `.assets` still get
  different storage classes without separate buckets — set per object at
  write time (§4.1), not via prefix-filtered lifecycle rules, which can't
  express "everything except `folder/`". The only thing that *forces* a
  bucket split is differing versioning, and versioning is now off for
  both, so there's no forcing function left. One bucket is simpler to
  operate (one policy, one set of credentials, one place to look).
- **Versioning: off**, bucket-wide. No longer in tension with anything,
  since backup is handled by scheduled sync rather than S3-native version
  history.
- **Backup: scheduled, periodic sync to Backblaze B2**, covering the whole
  bucket — both `.folder` and `.assets`. `.assets` is *not* fully
  regenerable from `.folder` (dashboard uploads exist only in Blot, see
  §2.2.2), so it needs the same protection. Backing up the whole bucket is
  simpler and less error-prone than enumerating which `.assets` subdirs are
  irreplaceable, at the cost of also copying regenerable thumbnails and
  image-cache files. **Decided over a
  same-provider (separate AWS account) destination**, specifically for true
  vendor independence: a separate AWS account still means "AWS" is the
  single company whose failure modes (account compromise, billing lockout,
  a bug in how you've configured cross-account access) could affect both
  copies at once. B2 is a fully separate company and infrastructure. Also
  decided over Bunny Storage (which, as of a 2026 update, is now genuinely
  S3-compatible too) for a related reason: Bunny is already your CDN
  vendor, so using it for backup too would mean a single Bunny-side problem
  could take out your CDN *and* your offsite copy simultaneously — the
  opposite of what "genuinely offsite" is meant to protect against. B2 is
  also the more S3-feature-complete option for this specific job (it
  supports lifecycle rules; Bunny's S3-compatible API currently doesn't),
  which matters for the retention-window approach below. This is close to
  what today's rolling EBS backups already provide, and matches the relaxed
  risk tolerance above. **Frequency: hourly, at worst** (your ceiling) —
  see §4.6 for exactly how this runs, what it costs, and §4.8 for a full
  current-vs-future cost comparison against today's EBS spend.
- **A dashboard "restore this deleted file" feature (next to entries in the
  sync history log), using S3 version history, was considered and
  shelved** — plausible if versioning were on, but you weren't confident
  it'd see enough use to justify the versioning cost/complexity it would
  require. Worth revisiting only if it comes up again as a real ask; it
  would mean turning versioning back on for at least the `folder/` prefix,
  which — now that `.folder` and `.assets` share a bucket — would mean
  either turning it on bucket-wide (paying the versioning cost on
  `.assets` too, for little benefit — its files are written once and never
  overwritten) or splitting back into two buckets after all. Worth
  remembering as the reason a future "let's add restore" ask isn't free.
- **No S3 Express One Zone.** Still applies for the same reasons as before
  (§4.2): single-AZ durability is a bad trade for the one copy of
  irreplaceable-ish data you have, and the SSD cache already targets the
  latency problem Express One Zone solves.
- **Cross-region for future multi-region compute: not needed at the storage
  layer.** Confirmed — app servers in any region should just read across
  the network from the single bucket's region, leaning on the SSD cache the
  same way same-region servers do. Live multi-region storage replication
  would add real consistency risk (async replication, no cross-region
  conflict resolution) that the current single-writer-per-blog lock model
  isn't built to handle, for a benefit you don't need yet.

### 2.2.1 The bucket can be public-read — worked through, and it holds up

You raised this because Blot's own user docs already state there's no
expectation of privacy for folder contents, and asked whether that
simplifies the Bunny question. It does, and the reasoning holds up under
scrutiny:

- **This isn't a new exposure, it's the same exposure Blot already has.**
  `app/cdn/index.js`'s `/folder/v-:version/...` route already serves any
  file in a blog's folder to anyone who requests it, unauthenticated, with
  no publish-status check — the only thing standing between "the internet"
  and a given file today is knowing the exact `blogID` + path. A public S3
  bucket with plain `GetObject` access doesn't remove or weaken that
  barrier; it's the same barrier.
- **That barrier is real, not security-by-obscurity theater, because
  `blogID` isn't guessable.** Confirmed in `app/models/blog/generateID.js`:
  a blog ID is `blog_` + a full UUIDv4 (128 bits of randomness), not a
  sequential or predictable value. So "must know the blogID" is a
  meaningful barrier against a stranger stumbling onto content, even though
  it's not a barrier against someone who already has a specific blog's ID
  and a path within it (which, again, is already true today).
- **The one guardrail worth keeping regardless: no `s3:ListBucket`.** Public
  `GetObject` on a known key is equivalent to today's exposure; public
  `ListBucket` would not be — it would let anyone enumerate every blogID and
  every file path in the bucket, which is a strictly new capability nobody
  has today (there's no equivalent "list all blogs" endpoint). This costs
  nothing to withhold and should be an explicit, deliberate part of the
  bucket policy, not an oversight either way.
- **"Public" must mean public-*read* only.** Write access stays exclusively
  through the app's IAM role (§2.3) — the bucket policy grants anonymous
  `GetObject`, never `PutObject`/`DeleteObject`. Worth stating plainly since
  "public bucket" could otherwise be misread as public read-write, which
  would let anyone overwrite or delete any blog's files.
- **One real exception, not a reason to reconsider, just a boundary to
  respect:** this reasoning applies to *blog folder content* — what a
  synced client actually writes, which is what "no expectation of privacy"
  in the user docs is about. It would not apply to Blot-internal bookkeeping
  data that happens to end up in the same bucket but isn't blog content in
  that sense. The clearest example: if git-backed sync is ever revisited
  (out of scope here, but §1's scope note left the door open to storing its
  data under `folder/{blogID}/gitClient/...`), a bare git repo's internals
  are Blot's own sync machinery, not "the folder contents" — they're a
  different thing than what the privacy statement covers, and shouldn't
  inherit this bucket's public-read policy by default. If/when that's
  revisited, it should get a genuinely private prefix or bucket, not just
  land under the public `folder/` prefix because it's convenient. Worth
  keeping in mind for the Phase 0/1 migration too: don't let any
  Blot-internal bookkeeping (lock files, partial-write temp files, etc.)
  leak into the public `folder/{blogID}/` key space — it should only ever
  contain what the sync clients actually wrote.

**How this simplifies Bunny (§4.4):** the previously-open question — does
Bunny support authenticated/signed origin requests to a private S3 bucket —
no longer needs an answer. A public bucket works with Bunny's plain,
documented Origin-URL-as-HTTPS-endpoint mechanism (exactly what their own S3
integration guide shows), so the direct-to-S3 optimization no longer has an
unresolved dependency on an unconfirmed Bunny feature.

### 2.2.2 `.assets` holds dashboard uploads too — not everything in it is regenerable

Template editor uploads and avatars reuse the `.assets` mechanism rather
than getting a scope of their own. Like the other files in `.assets`, they
never change once written (a replacement upload gets a new UUID filename),
are public, can be cached forever, and are fine on cheaper storage classes.
What makes them different is where they come from, not how they're stored:

| Subdir | Written by | Regenerable? |
|---|---|---|
| `_thumbnails` | `app/build/thumbnail/create.js` | Yes, from `.folder` |
| `_image_cache` | `app/build/plugins/image/optimize.js` | Yes, from `.folder` |
| `_assets` | document converters (`app/build/converters/*`) | Yes, from `.folder` |
| `_bookmark_screenshots` | `app/build/plugins/linkScreenshot` | Not faithfully — the linked page may have changed |
| `_avatars` | `app/dashboard/site/save/avatar.js` | **No** — exists only in Blot |
| `_template_assets` | template editor uploads (`app/dashboard/site/template/save/upload-{image,favicon,local}.js`) | **No** — exists only in Blot |

What follows from this:

- **Backed up with the rest of the bucket** (§2.2, §4.6), and the backup
  must be running before any `.assets` data is S3-only (§3, Phase 1).
- **Not counted against the user's quota** (§2.1) — unchanged.
- **Nothing may wipe the whole `.assets` scope** except blog deletion.
  Regeneration today empties named subdirs only (`app/sync/rebuild.js`
  runs `fs.emptyDir` on `_thumbnails` and `_image_cache`), and it must stay
  that way. The storage layer shouldn't expose a scope-wide delete for
  `.assets`, so a "clear and regenerate everything" operation can't be
  written by accident.
- **Template locals keep storing full CDN URLs** (e.g.
  `{config.cdn.origin}/{blogID}/_template_assets/{uuid}.webp`). Because
  `.assets` keys match these paths exactly (§2.3), nothing stored in Redis
  needs rewriting at migration time.
- **Deleting old files is uneven today.** `upload-image.js` and
  `upload-favicon.js` check every template of the blog before deleting a
  replaced file (duplicated templates share files); `upload-local.js` and
  `avatar.js` never delete the old file, so replaced uploads are orphaned.
  A future template-editor asset library (root `TODO`) would build on
  `_template_assets` in this scope rather than adding a new one.

### 2.3 Operational decisions

- **IAM: one role**, not scoped separately per prefix/scope. Simplest
  option; revisit only if a concrete need for narrower blast radius shows up.
- **Dev/test: a local S3-compatible mock (leaning MinIO) in the dev
  docker-compose stack, paired with `toxiproxy`** the same way Redis
  latency is already simulated today — mild preference, mainly to keep
  iterative work on PRs fast and dedupe hitting real AWS from CI/dev. MinIO
  is the default suggestion (widely adopted, actively maintained, easy
  Docker image) over alternatives like LocalStack (broader multi-service
  scope than needed here) or `s3rver` (lighter, but less actively
  maintained) — not a strongly-researched pick, just a reasonable default
  worth revisiting if it causes friction.
- **Separate buckets per environment** (dev/staging/prod), rather than one
  bucket with an environment-prefixed key space.
- **Object key layout: preserve existing per-blog relative paths 1:1**,
  rather than restructuring during migration. `.assets` keys are exactly
  today's public CDN paths — `{blogID}/_thumbnails/...`,
  `{blogID}/_template_assets/...` etc., no scope prefix — so no path
  rewriting is ever needed to serve them straight from the bucket (§4.4). `.folder` keys are `folder/{blogID}/{path}`.

### 2.4 Directory listing: paginated from day 1, no separate `.readdir()`

You asked whether the first pass should ship an unpaginated `.readdir()` to
be deprecated later in favor of a paginated `.list()`. Recommend skipping
that step entirely — go straight to a paginated `.list()` as the only
directory-listing primitive, even in Phase 0 before S3 exists. Building an
unpaginated method now, only to migrate every call site off it again later,
means paying the "visit every call site" cost twice for no benefit; better
to pay it once, now, while the abstraction is new and call sites are already
being touched.

**S3 side (confirmed this maps cleanly):** `ListObjectsV2` is natively
paginated — up to 1,000 keys per call, with an opaque
`NextContinuationToken` for the next page. Passing `Delimiter: "/"` groups
one-level-deep "subdirectories" into `CommonPrefixes` rather than flattening
every nested file, which is what gives filesystem-`readdir`-like semantics
(one level, not fully recursive) instead of a flat key dump. `.list()`'s
cursor can just be S3's own continuation token for that backend — no
reimplementation needed.

**Local-disk side, before S3 exists — also worth fixing now, not just
matching S3's shape:** today's crash risk (`app/blog/render/retrieve/folder.js`'s
live `fs.readdir`, per §1.2) comes specifically from `fs.readdir` buffering
an entire directory's entries into memory at once. Node's
`fs.promises.opendir()` gives an async-iterable directory handle that yields
entries one at a time without buffering the whole listing — implementing
the local backend's `.list()` on top of `opendir` (page-sized batches off
the iterator, cursor = how many entries consumed so far) actually starts
fixing the memory-crash risk immediately, in Phase 0, independent of
whether S3 has landed yet — not just a shape to match later.

**Proposed interface:**

```js
// Paginated primitive — the only listing method, safe for huge directories
const { entries, cursor } = await store.folder.list(path, { cursor, limit });
// cursor is opaque and backend-specific (S3's continuation token, or an
// opendir-position marker for local disk) — callers just pass it back

// Convenience wrapper for callers that genuinely need everything at once
// and can tolerate buffering it — implemented in terms of .list() above,
// looping until cursor is null
const allEntries = await store.folder.listAll(path);
```

`.listAll()` exists for callers like `buildFromFolder`'s directory scan or
the quota-reconciliation script, which really do need a full listing and can
afford to buffer it. The render-facing `folder` template local — the one
place a huge directory has actually caused problems — should be migrated to
call `.list()` directly with real pagination surfaced (or a hard cap),
never `.listAll()`, since buffering the whole thing there is the exact
failure mode being fixed.

**One open tradeoff, not yet resolved:** `opendir`'s streaming iteration
doesn't guarantee a stable sort order the way a pre-sorted array would, and
a meaningful opaque cursor generally wants a stable order to paginate
against safely. If the `folder` template local (or any other consumer)
needs deterministic (e.g. alphabetical) ordering, that's a real design
constraint that may require the local backend to still do more work than
pure unbuffered streaming — worth deciding explicitly rather than assuming
either "insertion order is fine" or "must be sorted" by default.

### 2.5 Case-insensitive path resolution: a separate, explicit method, not seamless

Today this logic exists at least twice, duplicated: `lookupFile.js`'s
case-insensitive fallback (its own 5,000-entry in-process cache, per §1.2)
and `helper/caseSensitivePath.js` (used by `app/blog/routes/assets.js` and
elsewhere). Recommend consolidating both into one storage-layer method,
exposed explicitly rather than built silently into `.read()`:

```js
// Explicit, opt-in — callers invoke this themselves on a miss
const resolvedPath = await store.folder.resolveCaseInsensitive(path);
```

**Why opt-in rather than seamless:** the vast majority of `.read()` calls
are for paths Blot itself generated and will always read back with identical
casing (template files, thumbnail keys, anything round-tripped internally) —
case mismatches are specifically a *user-authored-link* problem (someone
writing `![](image.JPG)` in a post when the real file is `image.jpg`), not a
general storage concern. Building the fallback into `.read()`'s hot path
would mean every internal caller silently carries the cost/complexity of a
capability only one class of caller (folder-link resolution, i.e. whatever
succeeds `replaceFolderLinks`/PR #1896's baking mechanism) actually needs —
against the "small, deliberate vocabulary" principle from §2.1. An explicit
method also makes it obvious at the call site that "this is specifically
working around user sloppiness," which is worth being visible in code
review.

**How it should work**, reusing pieces already decided elsewhere in this
plan:
1. Try the exact path first (equivalent to a plain `.exists()`/`.read()`).
2. On a miss, list the containing directory via `.list()` (§2.4) and find a
   case-insensitive match among the entries.
3. Cache the *resolved mapping* (requested path → actual path, or "no
   match") aggressively, keyed by `(blogID, blog.cacheID, requestedPath)` —
   reusing the exact invalidation trigger (`cacheID` bump on any blog
   change) the render pipeline already relies on elsewhere
   (`full-view-cache.js`, `lookupFile.js`'s own cache), so this doesn't
   invent a new invalidation convention.
4. **Back that cache with Redis, not just an in-process `Map`.** An
   in-process cache only helps whichever single app instance happened to
   resolve a given path first; once multiple app boxes exist (§1.3, §4.3),
   every other instance would redundantly redo the same directory listing.
   Redis is already the established place for small, bounded metadata like
   this (§1.8) — the resolved-path cache fits that description exactly.
5. Scope this to `.folder` only — `.assets` paths are always
   Blot-generated and read back by Blot itself, so case mismatches can't
   structurally occur there; there's nothing to resolve.

### 2.6 SSD cache invalidation: per-object, not per-blog — and why `cacheID` is the wrong trigger for this specific cache

You raised this as needing "some kind of content hash tracking" — the
diagnosis is right, and it's worth separating two different problems that
"cacheID" conflates elsewhere in this codebase:

- The render caches (`full-view-cache.js`, `lookupFile.js`) invalidate
  everything for a blog on any `cacheID` bump because a single file change
  *could*, in principle, affect any rendered page (via includes, archives,
  menus, etc.) — a full dependency graph would be needed to invalidate more
  precisely, so blanket invalidation is a reasonable, deliberate tradeoff
  there.
- The SSD cache is different: it holds raw file *bytes*, and one file's
  bytes have zero bearing on whether another file's cached bytes are still
  valid. Tying SSD cache invalidation to `cacheID` would mean a sync that
  touches a single file forces eviction of the *entire* blog's cached
  working set on next access — exactly the problem you're flagging, and
  strictly unnecessary given the cache is keyed per-file already.

**The fix falls out of the abstraction itself: since every write already
goes through the storage layer, the write path knows exactly which single
object just changed — invalidation can be precise for free, not detected
after the fact.** Two mechanisms working together, for the two different
staleness sources:

1. **Same-instance writes — direct, immediate, no cache-check needed.**
   When `storage.for(blogID).folder.write(path, data)` runs on a given
   box, that box's own local SSD cache entry for `(blogID, path)` gets
   updated (or evicted) directly, right there, since the new content is
   already in hand. Zero extra I/O, zero reliance on a hash or timestamp.
2. **Cross-instance writes — the real reason "content hash tracking" is
   needed.** Once multiple app boxes exist (§1.3, §4.3), each with its own
   local SSD cache, a write on box A doesn't automatically inform box B's
   cache. Recommend extending the pub/sub broadcast that already happens at
   the end of every sync — `app/sync/index.js` already does
   `client.publish("blog:" + blogID + ":preview:reload", "reload")` when a
   sync changes something — to also carry *which specific paths* changed,
   not just "this blog changed." Every app instance subscribes and evicts
   (or refreshes) exactly those SSD cache entries. This reuses
   infrastructure and a pattern that already exists at exactly the right
   point in the code, rather than inventing a new one, and gives per-object
   granularity, not per-blog.
3. **A content-hash safety net, for the case pub/sub misses** (an instance
   was down during the publish, a message was dropped, etc.): store each
   object's S3 ETag (a content hash, given to you for free on every S3
   PUT/GET/HEAD response) alongside its SSD-cached copy. A periodic
   reconciliation sweep — similar in spirit to the quota reconciliation
   already planned in §3 — can spot-check cached entries' ETags against S3
   and evict mismatches, catching the rare missed-invalidation case without
   making every read pay a validation cost.

This means "content hash tracking" ends up as a background safety net, not
the primary invalidation mechanism — the primary mechanism is the pub/sub
broadcast, which is cheaper (no per-read S3 HEAD check) and more immediate.

---

## 3. What a storage abstraction should look like, given the above

This tracks your own `TODO` sketch closely, refined by the decisions above:

```
app/storage/
  index.js   # storage.for(blogID) -> { folder: {...}, assets: {...} }
             # storage.folder.*(blogID, ...) / storage.assets.*(blogID, ...) as flat wrappers
```

- **Phase −1, a hard prerequisite, not part of this abstraction's own
  work:** land PR #1896 (or its approach extended to any remaining
  live-render-time folder-link lookups) — see the sequencing note at the
  top of this doc. Don't start Phase 0 while this is still in flight.
- **Phase 0 (do this regardless of S3):** implement the abstraction as a thin
  wrapper over today's `fs-extra` calls against local disk, with *no behavior
  change*, and migrate the ~30+ direct call sites onto it. This alone pays for
  itself: it's the only way to add per-blog size accounting (§1.5) without a
  disk scan, and it's the prerequisite for everything else. Low risk, pure
  refactor, testable file-by-file. Includes building `.list()` paginated
  from day 1 (§2.4) — the local-disk `opendir`-backed implementation starts
  fixing today's huge-directory crash risk immediately, before S3 is even
  in the picture — and a small, short-TTL, in-process write-through
  memoization scoped to a single sync operation (§4.9), so a `write()`
  followed shortly by a `read()` of the same path within one
  `folder.update()` doesn't pay a redundant round trip. This is
  independent of, and much cheaper than, the Phase 2b decision below.
- **Phase 1 — `.assets` first**, not blog folders. These files are
  mostly written asynchronously at build time (§1.2), all content-addressed
  or UUID-keyed, and never change once written — the simplest shape to move.
  Unlike thumbnails, though, the dashboard uploads in this scope
  (`_template_assets`, `_avatars`, §2.2.2) can't be regenerated, so **the B2
  backup (§4.6) must be running before local disk stops holding a copy of
  `.assets`**, not added afterwards. Dual-write to
  S3 + local disk, read-through with the local copy as cache, and (per
  §4.4) issue a Bunny cache-warm request in the same async step so the
  first real visitor doesn't pay a cold miss. Confirms your own instinct in
  `TODO` ("we probably don't need a caching layer for static files since
  static files will end up in CDN storage after first request").
- **Phase 2a — blog folder source files, S3-direct, deliberately *without*
  the SSD cache at first.** Move `.folder` to S3 as the source of truth,
  with no local caching layer yet, and measure real latency/throughput in
  production against actual traffic before building anything more. See
  §4.9 for why this has a real chance of being sufficient on its own — if
  it holds up, Phase 2b (the cache) may not be needed at all, which is a
  meaningfully simpler system (no eviction policy, no cross-instance
  invalidation problem in §2.6, nothing to keep consistent). This phase
  must also replace `folder.js`'s live per-request `readdir`/`stat` (§1.2)
  with `.list()` (§2.4), land the consolidated case-insensitive resolution
  method (§2.5), **and parallelize `rebuild.js`'s serial
  `async.eachSeries` file-processing loop (§4.9)** — this last one is a
  real, confirmed-in-code risk independent of the caching decision, and
  needs fixing before `.folder` moves to S3, not after. Also the natural
  place to add the sampled shadow-read instrumentation (§4.9) that turns
  the Phase 2b decision into a measurement rather than a guess.
- **Phase 2b — the SSD-LRU-cache pattern from §1.4, generalized from the
  macserver watcher, only if Phase 2a's measurements show it's actually
  needed.** If built: source of truth stays S3; local SSD holds a working
  set, evicted least-recently-used. Read path never blocks on S3 for a warm
  blog; a cold blog pays a one-time fetch before its next sync/render, not
  per-request. This is where the per-object cache invalidation design
  (§2.6) would land, if this phase happens at all.
- **Alongside all of this:** replace the local-disk `proper-lockfile` lock
  (§1.3) with a Redis-based distributed lock before ever running multiple app
  boxes against the same S3 bucket — otherwise two boxes syncing the same blog
  concurrently will corrupt state exactly as the current lock is designed to
  prevent.
- **Quotas** become nearly free once the abstraction is the sole write path:
  track cumulative bytes per blog as a counter (Redis or the blog's DB
  record), check against a cap before accepting a `.folder` write (§2.1),
  reject with a clear "storage quota exceeded" error surfaced to the sync
  client (this is exactly the still-open `TODO` items in §1.5). Periodic
  reconciliation (recompute from S3 `ListObjects`/local `du` occasionally)
  guards against counter drift from partial failures.

---

## 4. S3-specific plan

### 4.1 Bucket-type and object-settings summary (as requested)

**Monobucket — the recommended, decided shape (§2.2):**

| | `.folder`: `folder/{blogID}/...` | `.assets`: `{blogID}/...` (bucket root) |
|---|---|---|
| Bucket type | One general-purpose (regional) S3 bucket — not a Directory Bucket / Express One Zone | same bucket |
| Versioning | Off | Off |
| Storage class at write | Standard | Intelligent-Tiering, set on each `PutObject` (Bunny permacaches almost immediately, so repeat direct reads become rare) |
| Lifecycle rules | None | None — the storage class is set at write time instead, since `.assets` has no key prefix to filter on |
| Backup | Hourly sync to Backblaze B2 (§4.6) | Same sync — whole bucket. Dashboard uploads can't be regenerated (§2.2.2) |
| Quota enforcement | Yes, user-facing cap | Tracked, not enforced against the user |

**Why Intelligent-Tiering and not Standard-IA for `.assets`:** Standard-IA
bills every object as at least 128KB and at least 30 days of storage. Many
thumbnails and template uploads are smaller than 128KB, so IA could cost
*more* than Standard for them. Intelligent-Tiering has no minimum object
size or retrieval fees; objects under 128KB are never monitored or moved
(they're billed at the Standard rate, with no per-object monitoring fee),
and larger ones move to cheaper tiers automatically once they stop being
read.

**Dual-bucket — the earlier alternative, now not the default, kept here for
reference in case requirements change (e.g. the "local"-client caveat in
§2.2 turns out to matter, or the restore-from-version feature becomes a
real ask):**

| | `.folder` bucket | `.assets` bucket |
|---|---|---|
| Bucket type | General-purpose S3 | General-purpose S3 |
| Versioning | On, with a lifecycle rule expiring noncurrent versions after a retention window | Off |
| Cross-Region Replication | On, to a cross-account/cross-region replica (requires versioning, which is why this shape needs two buckets) | Off |
| Storage class | Standard | Intelligent-Tiering |

The dual-bucket shape is strictly more protective (near-real-time offsite
copy instead of periodic sync, plus point-in-time version recovery) but
costs more in complexity and, per your steer, isn't needed given the
"users have their own copy" reframe. Switching from monobucket to
dual-bucket later is possible but not trivial — it's a real migration, not
a config flag — so worth being reasonably confident in the monobucket
choice before building on it.

### 4.2 Why not S3 Express One Zone

Yes — it's a bucket-level choice (technically a distinct "directory bucket"
type you create up front, not a per-object storage class within a normal
bucket). Recommend against it:

- It's **single-AZ** — lower durability than Standard's multi-AZ
  replication. Even with the relaxed "acceptable to lose" framing in §2.2,
  there's no reason to add unnecessary durability risk on top of an
  already-reduced backup posture.
- Its main benefit — single-digit-millisecond per-request latency — targets
  exactly the problem the SSD LRU cache (§1.4, §3 Phase 2) is already
  designed to solve. If the cache is doing its job, most reads never reach
  S3 at all.
- For `.assets`, the case is weaker still: Bunny permacaches this content
  after first fetch, so origin request latency for it is close to
  irrelevant already.
- It adds real complexity (directory-bucket API differences, single-AZ
  pinning, VPC endpoint requirements) for a benefit that's largely
  redundant with a design choice already made for other reasons.

Worth revisiting only if a real benchmark (§4.5) shows Standard's latency is
a genuine bottleneck even with the SSD cache warm — unlikely given the
design.

### 4.3 Multi-region compute, separately from multi-region storage

Confirmed: no live storage replication needed to support future
multi-region app servers. Keep the bucket single-region as the source of
truth; app servers in any region read across the network from that one
region, with the SSD LRU cache absorbing the extra latency the same way it
already absorbs S3 latency for same-region servers. This avoids a real
correctness risk active-active multi-region storage would introduce: S3
replication is asynchronous with no cross-region conflict resolution, so
two regions writing to the same blog around the same time could see stale
reads during the replication lag window — and the current
single-writer-per-blog lock model (even once moved to Redis, per §1.3)
isn't designed to reason about that across regions. If genuine multi-region
*active* storage is ever needed later, AWS's Multi-Region Access Points
(built for exactly this) is the mechanism to reach for rather than
hand-rolling it — a future problem, not one to solve now.

### 4.4 Bunny CDN — plan, Perma-Cache warming, and the direct-to-S3 path

**Confirmed against your actual setup:** origin is the Blot app server,
no significant Edge Rules today, Origin Shield is enabled.

**Recommendation, unchanged: don't reconfigure Bunny for the initial
migration.** `app/cdn/index.js` and `app/blog/routes/assets.js` keep serving
the same routes at the same paths; the storage layer just swaps what backs
them (S3 + SSD cache instead of `express.static()` on local disk). Origin
Shield keeps working exactly as it does today regardless of what's behind
the app server. Zero Bunny-side changes required for the core migration.

**Perma-Cache researched in detail, since you asked about seeding it
directly from the storage layer:**

- Perma-Cache is a real, named Bunny feature: a storage-zone-backed layer
  that sits between the edge cache and your origin. On a cache miss, Bunny
  checks Perma-Cache first; if absent there too, it fetches from your
  origin and populates Perma-Cache *in the background*. Once cached, an
  entry never evicts on TTL/LRU — only an explicit purge removes it.
- Bunny stores Perma-Cache entries internally at a synthetic,
  Bunny-managed path inside the linked storage zone:
  `/__bcdn_perma_cache__/pullzone__<name>__<unique_id>/<request-path>/___filename___/___file___`
  — not a plain mirror of your object keys. Critically, the `<unique_id>`
  segment **rotates on a full-zone purge**, silently orphaning anything
  placed under the old id.
- **Bunny's own docs don't document a supported way to directly upload
  into Perma-Cache to pre-seed it** — the only documented population path
  is the miss → origin-fetch → background-populate flow. Given the opaque,
  purge-mutable internal path scheme above, writing into it directly would
  mean reverse-engineering an undocumented, mutable internal format —
  fragile, and liable to silently break on a Bunny-side change.
- **Recommendation instead of a direct dual-write:** after writing a newly
  generated asset to S3, have the same async build-time step issue one
  deliberate HTTP GET to the asset's public CDN URL. This triggers exactly
  the same miss → fetch → Perma-Cache-populate flow Bunny already
  documents and supports — the *first real visitor* then already hits a
  warm Perma-Cache entry instead of a cold miss. This is the officially
  supported mechanism, costs one extra request in a context that's already
  async and already paying the "cost at build time, not request time"
  price (§1.2), and requires no knowledge of Bunny's internal storage
  format at all. This is the answer to "can the storage layer dual-write
  directly to Perma-Cache" — not directly, but a one-line HTTP warm-up call
  achieves the same outcome through the supported path.

**Object path structure, and serving straight from S3 later:**

Today's CDN-facing URLs map to a bare `{blogID}/{relativePath}` for both
scopes, with no scope-identifying prefix (§1.1) — the `/folder/v-X/`
segment is stripped by Express before touching disk, and the assets route
is mounted at the CDN root.

**`.assets` keys are identical to its public URL paths — no Edge Rules or
path rewriting needed.** The S3 key for
`https://{cdn}/{blogID}/_template_assets/{uuid}.webp` is exactly
`{blogID}/_template_assets/{uuid}.webp` (§2.2, §2.3). The URLs already
stored in template locals and entry HTML stay valid as they are, and S3
can answer for them without any rewriting.

**The catch: `.assets` shares a Bunny pull zone (hostname) with routes that
S3 can't answer.** `/folder/v-{version}/{blogID}/...` carries a
cache-busting segment that isn't part of any key, and `/template/...`
(rendered CSS/JS, §1.2) isn't in the bucket at all. So Bunny's origin can't
simply be swapped to the bucket for the whole zone. For now the plan is:

- **The app server stays the origin for everything.** The core migration
  needs no Bunny changes at all: `app/cdn/index.js` and
  `app/blog/routes/assets.js` serve the same URLs, reading from S3 (plus
  cache) instead of local disk.
- **If direct-to-S3 serving is ever wanted**, it needs either a
  path-*routing* rule (send `/folder/*` and `/template/*` to the app,
  everything else to the bucket — no rewriting) or a separate hostname for
  `.assets`. Either way, the key layout means no rewriting is ever needed
  for `.assets`. `.folder` would need its `v-{version}` segment dropped
  (a real rewrite), so it stays served through the app — see §5.

Origin Shield stays valuable and needs no change either way — it reduces
origin requests the same way whether the origin is the app or S3.

**The one open dependency this had — whether Bunny supports signed/
authenticated requests to a private bucket — is now moot.** Per §2.2.1 the
bucket is public-read by decision, which is exactly the shape Bunny's own
documented S3 integration already expects (a plain HTTPS Origin URL against
a public bucket, no signing).

### 4.6 Backup mechanics and cost, for the hourly whole-bucket sync to Backblaze B2

**Decided: Backblaze B2, via its S3-compatible API**, not a second AWS
bucket — see §2.2's reasoning (genuine vendor independence from both AWS and
Bunny). Confirmed this works exactly as hoped: the AWS CLI's `aws s3 sync`
accepts `--endpoint-url` to target *any* S3-compatible service, using that
provider's own credentials instead of AWS IAM. Concretely:

```
aws s3 sync s3://blot-primary-bucket/ s3://blot-backup/ \
  --endpoint-url https://s3.<region>.backblazeb2.com \
  --profile backblaze
```

where the `backblaze` profile holds a B2 Application Key ID/Key (created in
the B2 console, scoped to a single bucket if you want to limit blast
radius), not AWS credentials — the source side of the command still reads
from the primary AWS bucket using your normal AWS credentials, so this is a
cross-provider copy, not a same-provider one.

This covers the whole bucket, not just `folder/`: `.assets` sits at the
bucket root and includes dashboard uploads that can't be regenerated
(§2.2.2).

**How this runs, entirely outside the application codebase** — same two
options as before, unaffected by the destination change:
- **A cron entry** (existing EC2 host, or a small dedicated instance)
  running the command above hourly. Blot already has cron usage in its ops
  patterns (per `TODO`'s "copy the cron tab into the new server setup
  script" item), so this fits an existing pattern.
- **A scheduled AWS Lambda** (hourly via EventBridge Scheduler), reimplementing
  the sync/diff logic via the AWS SDK rather than relying on the CLI (Lambda
  doesn't ship the `aws` binary) — genuinely serverless, no host to
  maintain, worth it mainly if "no dependency on any specific host" matters
  more than simplicity.

**Delete semantics — decided: grow-and-expire, and it's fully achievable on
B2, not just a fallback if versioning happened to be available.** Confirmed
directly: **B2 buckets are always versioned by default** ("The buckets that
are in Backblaze B2 Cloud Storage are always versioned by default" — this
isn't a toggle, every bucket works this way, on any API). B2's
S3-compatible API also supports S3-style Lifecycle Rules, including
`NoncurrentVersionExpiration` (delete old, superseded versions after N
days) and plain `Expiration` (delete current objects after N days),
filterable by key prefix. Put together, this gives real grow-and-expire
semantics, not just "don't delete":

- Run `aws s3 sync` **without `--delete`**. When a file's content changes,
  the sync uploads new content to the same key — because the bucket is
  always versioned, the old content becomes a *noncurrent* version rather
  than being destroyed, and the current version always reflects the latest
  sync. When a file is removed from the primary, sync simply leaves the
  corresponding key untouched in the backup (protects against accidental
  deletion).
- Add a `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` lifecycle rule (e.g. `NoncurrentDays:
  60–90`, bucket-wide) so superseded versions — the
  actual protection against an accidental *overwrite* or corruption, not
  just a delete — get cleaned up automatically after the retention window,
  rather than accumulating forever.
- **Note the important distinction this reveals**: plain `Expiration`
  (deleting *current* objects after N days) would be the wrong rule to
  apply broadly here — a blog post's image that simply hasn't changed in
  years is still current, valid content, and shouldn't expire just because
  it's old. Only `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` (which only ever touches
  *superseded* versions, never the current one) is the right mechanism for
  this use case.

**Switching between grow-and-expire and a pure `--delete` mirror later, on
the same existing bucket, is easy — confirmed no bucket recreation is
needed either direction.** Versioning is inherent to every B2 bucket
regardless of which sync mode you use, and lifecycle rules are ordinary
bucket configuration, addable/changeable/removable at any time via the
standard S3 `PutBucketLifecycleConfiguration`/`DeleteBucketLifecycleConfiguration`
calls. Switching is really just: change the `--delete` flag on your sync
command, and add or remove the lifecycle rule — no migration, no
downtime, nothing structural. The only practical thing to be aware of:
switching *into* `--delete` mode after running grow-and-expire for a while
means the very next sync run will remove every backup object that's no
longer present in the primary, which could be a meaningful one-time cleanup
if a lot has been deleted from the primary over time — not a technical
blocker, just worth expecting.

**Cost drivers** (worked through against your actual data volume in a
private calculation, not reproduced here — see the note at the end of §4.8):
- **B2 storage**: $6/TB-month ($0.006/GB-month) — roughly a quarter of S3
  Standard's per-GB price, growing slowly as the backup accumulates changed
  objects (bounded by the retention window above, not unbounded).
- **B2 ingress (receiving the synced data)**: **free** — B2 does not charge
  for data uploaded into it, regardless of source.
- **AWS side — this is the cost that's easy to miss**: getting data *out*
  of S3 to a non-AWS destination is billed as standard internet egress from
  S3, since Backblaze isn't part of any AWS-side free-transfer arrangement.
  AWS's standard tier: first 100GB/month of internet egress free
  (account-wide, shared with anything else leaving via the internet), then
  ~$0.09/GB up to 10TB/month. This is the one line item that exists
  specifically *because* the backup destination is a different cloud
  provider — the same copy to a second AWS account would have been a free
  server-side `CopyObject` instead. It's the real, small price of the
  vendor-independence decision, not a mistake to optimize away.
- **One-time cost, not recurring**: the initial full sync has to push
  the whole bucket out of S3 once, at the same per-GB egress rate
  above (minus whatever the free monthly tier absorbs).
- **Compute**: negligible either way (cron invocation or Lambda), same as
  before.

See §4.8 for how this fits into the full current-vs-future picture (kept
directional/percentage-only there, by design — see the note at the end of
that section).

### 4.7 Still to verify (not answerable from this codebase)

- **Latency and throughput under real conditions** — see the how-to below;
  needed to size the SSD cache's hit-rate target realistically before
  writing storage-layer code.
- **Exact retention window** for the B2 backup's grow-and-expire
  `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` rule (§4.6) — a number (60? 90?), not
  otherwise decided.

**B2 region: decided.** Your AWS instance is in `us-west-2` (Oregon).
Backblaze's regions are US West (Sacramento + Phoenix data centers), US
East (Reston, VA), EU Central (Amsterdam), and Canada East. **US West** is
both the closest to Oregon and satisfies "US-located at minimum." One thing
worth knowing before creating the B2 account: **the region is a permanent,
account-level choice — it cannot be changed after account creation**, so
this is a one-way door, not a per-bucket setting you can revisit later
without opening a new B2 account.

**How to gather the two data points still needed, both from the AWS Console
dashboard — no CLI setup required for either:**

- **Actual `.folder`/`.assets` data volume**: you already have this (the
  `du -sh` figures you gave me) — nothing further needed here.
- **Daily write rate** (for the B2 sync cost estimate) and **EBS
  read/write latency and throughput** (for sizing the SSD cache): both come
  from the same place — **EC2 Console → Elastic Block Store → Volumes →
  select your volume → Monitoring tab**, which graphs CloudWatch's native
  per-volume EBS metrics with no setup required (they're collected
  automatically for every EBS volume, no agent to install):
  - `VolumeWriteBytes` summed over a day ≈ your daily write rate (this
    measures bytes written to the block device, which is a reasonable
    upper-bound proxy for "new data" — it'll run a bit high vs. actual
    unique changed file bytes, since filesystem metadata/journaling writes
    count too, which is fine for a worst-case estimate).
  - `VolumeReadBytes`/`VolumeWriteBytes` per period ÷ period length =
    throughput (bytes/sec).
  - `VolumeReadOps`/`VolumeWriteOps` per period ÷ period length = IOPS.
  - **Average per-operation latency isn't a metric by itself** — AWS's
    documented way to derive it is `VolumeTotalReadTime ÷ VolumeReadOps`
    (and the write equivalent), giving average seconds-per-operation over
    the period. The Monitoring tab's console graphs typically show the raw
    components; you may need CloudWatch's metrics explorer directly
    (CloudWatch Console → Metrics → EBS → Per-Volume Metrics) to plot the
    derived ratio, or just read off the two raw numbers for a given period
    and divide by hand.
  - **One real gap: there's no "list" (directory listing) latency metric.**
    EBS/CloudWatch only sees block-device I/O, not filesystem-level
    operations — a directory listing is invisible to it as a distinct
    thing, it's just more read I/O. Isolating "how long does `readdir` on
    our biggest directories actually take" needs to be measured at the
    application/OS level directly (e.g., timing `fs.readdir` calls in the
    app, or `strace -T`/`iostat` on the host) rather than pulled from a
    dashboard — worth doing as a quick one-off script/log rather than
    expecting AWS to have already collected it.
  - All of this is 15 months of retained history by default, so you can
    pull a representative recent week or month without setting anything up
    in advance. CLI equivalents exist (`aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics
    --namespace AWS/EBS ...`) if you'd rather export the raw numbers for me
    to work with directly later, but the console graphs should answer both
    questions without needing that.

### 4.8 Cost estimate: current EBS spend vs. after migration

A full line-item cost comparison (current EBS volume + snapshot spend vs.
S3 + reduced-EBS + B2 backup spend after migration) was worked through
directly with you, using your actual data volume and current AWS spend.
That comparison, and the underlying size/dollar figures, is deliberately
**not reproduced in this doc** — those numbers indirectly reveal userbase
scale, which is treated as private information, separately from everything
else in this research (which is about architecture, not business metrics).

**Directional result, safe to state:** the migration is projected to reduce
combined storage + backup spend by **roughly 80%**. The main structural
reasons behind that number (which don't depend on the specific figures) are
worth keeping in the plan:
- EBS gp2 bills for the full *provisioned* volume size regardless of how
  much is actually used — exactly the "no more charges for unused GB disk
  space" problem your own `TODO` note names as a goal here. S3 bills for
  actual data stored.
- EBS snapshot storage bills for blocks that have *ever* been written to
  the volume (unless TRIM/discard reclaims them), not just what's currently
  live on the filesystem — so snapshot cost today likely reflects
  historical churn (deleted files, regenerated assets, old builds) that
  isn't providing any value now. B2's backup cost is based on what's
  actually being mirrored today, so this "paying to back up data you no
  longer have" effect goes away structurally, not just because B2's
  per-GB rate happens to be cheaper.
- The one cost that exists specifically *because* the backup destination is
  a different cloud provider (AWS→B2 egress, §4.6) is real but small
  relative to the storage-cost savings — the vendor-independence decision
  isn't free, but it isn't what determines the overall number either way.

If a fuller cost breakdown is useful again later, it's best treated as a
private calculation between us rather than something that lives in this
document.

### 4.9 Can `.folder` skip the SSD cache entirely? Worth measuring before building it

You asked whether it's worth sizing up pure S3-backed latency/throughput
*before* building the caching layer, on the chance it turns out unnecessary.
Strongly agree this is worth doing first, not as an afterthought — and there
are concrete reasons to think it has a real chance of working, not just
optimism:

**The read paths that would actually hit S3 directly are narrower than
"every file read" once everything else in this plan lands:**
- Rendered HTML/CSS/JS never touches raw file storage at all — it's
  Redis-cached (§1.2).
- Generated assets are permacached by Bunny after first fetch (§1.7, §4.4),
  and the plan already has the storage layer actively warming that cache at
  build time (§4.4) rather than waiting for a real visitor to trigger the
  first fetch.
- Origin Shield (confirmed enabled, §4.4) already deduplicates concurrent
  cache misses across edge nodes into a single origin request, rather than
  every miss independently hitting the origin.
- Folder-link resolution and the `folder` template local are being
  restructured anyway (build-time baking via PR #1896, paginated `.list()`
  via §2.4) — independent of whether a cache exists underneath them.
- Sync/build-time file reads (converting a doc, generating a thumbnail) are
  async and already off any visitor's critical path (§1.2) — S3 latency
  there is close to irrelevant no matter what it is.

What's left, once all of that is accounted for, is genuinely just: **a
Bunny cache-miss that needs a fresh fetch from your origin for a
`.folder`/`.assets` file.** That's a real request, but it's a *cache-fill*
event, not a per-visitor request — Bunny's own tolerance for how long an
origin fetch takes is generous (it's filling its own cache once, not
serving a page load directly), so the bar "pure S3 needs to clear" is lower
than "must feel instant to a visitor."

**Rough scale of what that bar actually is, from published S3 figures (not
derived from your setup — treat as ballpark, to be replaced by your own
measurement):** same-region S3 GET latency is typically single-to-low-double-digit
milliseconds at p50 (roughly 10-20ms), with a heavier tail at p99 depending
on object size and request patterns — meaningfully higher than gp2 EBS's
typical ~1-2ms per operation (worth remembering gp2 is *also*
network-attached block storage over AWS's Nitro fabric, not physically
local disk, so this isn't a "network vs. local" comparison, just a "how much
protocol/routing overhead per request" one). For a single cache-fill fetch,
an extra ~10-20ms is very likely imperceptible; the real question is
whether *any* request pattern in this system is latency-sensitive enough,
or frequent enough at volume, for that difference to matter in aggregate —
which is exactly what needs measuring, not assumed either way.

**One case pure S3 could genuinely struggle with — confirmed in code, not
speculative, and worth flagging as a real risk to design around:** a full
rebuild of a many-thousand-file blog processes files strictly one at a
time. `app/sync/rebuild.js:113-124` walks the entire blog folder, then runs
`async.eachSeries(updatePaths, ...)` — every changed path's `update(path,
...)` call runs to completion before the next one starts, for the whole
blog. Each `update()` call very likely does at least one file read
internally (hashing, content parsing, thumbnail/build triggers). Under S3,
that's thousands of files × a per-request latency serially, with zero
overlap — a rebuild that takes seconds today could plausibly take minutes.
**A local SSD cache wouldn't fix this either** — a full rebuild touches
every file for the first time, so the cache starts stone cold regardless of
whether Phase 2b exists. The actual lever is **parallelizing this loop**
(S3 comfortably handles high concurrent request rates — official guidance
is 5,500 GET/HEAD requests/sec *per prefix*, and `blogID`-prefixed keys
already spread load across many prefixes), not caching. This needs to be
addressed as part of the S3 migration itself, independent of the Phase 2a/2b
caching decision — worth auditing `rebuild.js` and `buildFromFolder.js` for
this specifically before cutting `.folder` over to S3, not discovering it
after.

**A related, but genuinely different problem, worth pulling apart
explicitly: the ordinary sync → build flow, not just full rebuilds.** A
regular file-change-via-client-sync-then-build flow can plausibly read the
*same* just-written file's content more than once in quick succession
within one `folder.update(path)` call — computing a hash/fingerprint,
parsing content, triggering a thumbnail/build step, etc., potentially each
independently calling `.read()` on data the same process just handled
moments ago. This is a different shape of problem than Phase 2b's design
target (a long-lived, cross-instance, multi-blog cache serving *unrelated*
future requests) — it's narrow, single-operation, single-process, and
short-lived. Recommend **not** conflating the two: rather than concluding
"therefore Phase 2b must exist," a much cheaper mechanism handles this
specific case — a small, short-TTL, in-process write-through memoization
inside the storage layer itself, scoped to a single sync operation (e.g.
cleared at the end of `folder.update`, or just a few seconds' TTL keyed by
`(blogID, path)`). When `store.folder.write(path, data)` runs, the content
is already in hand — an immediately-following `.read()` for that same path,
within the same operation, can be served from that short-lived memoization
instead of a fresh S3 round trip, with none of Phase 2b's complexity
(no eviction policy, no cross-instance consistency problem, since it's
deliberately not meant to outlive one sync operation). This is cheap enough
to build in Phase 0/1 regardless of what Phase 2a's measurements eventually
say about the bigger cache — it solves a structurally different problem
(redundant round trips *within* one logical operation) than Phase 2b solves
(serving *unrelated* future requests from a warm cache). Worth pairing this
with an actual audit of the sync/build pipeline for how many independent
`.read()` calls a single file change triggers today — the cheapest fix of
all might just be threading the content already read once through the rest
of the pipeline in memory, rather than re-reading it via the storage layer
at each step, which the memoization above is a safety net for, not a
replacement for fixing at the source where practical.

**Recommended approach for the *actual* Phase 2b question, now with a
better source of data than a synthetic benchmark:** you suggested
instrumenting real read operations during the dual-write phase (Phase 1)
to get concrete X→Y timing numbers rather than synthetic ones — this is a
stronger version of the `toxiproxy` benchmark idea from §4.7, not just a
supplement to it, since it measures actual production access patterns
rather than an approximation. Concretely: during Phase 1/2a, have a
*sampled* fraction of reads (e.g. 1-5%, not every read — keeping the
measurement's own S3 request-cost overhead negligible) perform a shadow S3
read alongside the real (local-disk, in Phase 1; or the only read, once
cut over in 2a) read, timing both, and log
`{operation, scope, byteSize, localMs, s3Ms}` — either to existing app logs
(queryable via the existing SSH log tooling) or a small Redis-backed
rolling histogram, whichever is cheaper to query later. Since every read
already funnels through the storage abstraction (that's the whole point of
building it first), this is a natural, single-choke-point place to add the
instrumentation — no need to touch call sites individually. This gives real
X-was-N-ms/Y-would-have-been-M-ms numbers for your actual traffic and file-size
distribution, which is a strictly better input to the Phase 2b decision
than a synthetic `toxiproxy` benchmark — recommend leaning on this over the
synthetic version once Phase 1 is running, and using `toxiproxy` mainly for
earlier, pre-Phase-1 sanity-checking before any real traffic exists to
measure.

## 5. Other open decisions worth resolving before/while building

- **Whether Phase 2b (the SSD cache) needs to be built at all** (§4.9) —
  the single biggest open question left in this plan, and the best kind to
  have: resolvable by measurement (the shadow-read instrumentation below)
  rather than judgment call. Everything else in §2.6 (cross-instance
  invalidation, ETag reconciliation) is moot if the answer turns out to be
  "no."
- **`rebuild.js`'s serial file-processing loop** (§4.9) — confirmed real,
  not speculative (`async.eachSeries` at `app/sync/rebuild.js:113`). Needs
  a concrete parallelization approach decided (bounded concurrency via
  `async.eachLimit` or similar, sized to avoid overwhelming S3's per-prefix
  request-rate guidance) before `.folder` moves to S3.
- **Shadow-read instrumentation sampling rate** (§4.9) — what fraction of
  reads to double up during Phase 1/2a (1%? 5%?) to gather real S3-vs-local
  timing data without adding meaningful S3 request-cost overhead from the
  measurement itself.
- **B2 retention window** (§4.7) — a number for the
  `NoncurrentVersionExpiration` rule, not otherwise decided.
- **Daily write rate and EBS latency/throughput** (§4.7) — data-gathering
  steps given, not yet pulled.
- **Object key layout — one thing still worth a check**: S3 keys are always
  case-sensitive (unlike some filesystem configurations Blot has had to
  work around historically) — this *removes* a storage-backend quirk rather
  than adding one, and it's now clear the case-insensitive resolution
  method (§2.5) exists specifically for ambiguous user-facing links, not as
  a storage-layer workaround. Still worth a one-time scan of existing
  filenames for anything that's safe on POSIX but could behave oddly as a
  raw S3 key (leading/trailing whitespace, control characters) before
  migration, as a sanity check rather than a redesign.
- **Direct-to-S3 serving for `.folder`, if ever** (§4.4) — `.assets` keys
  match its public URLs, but `.folder` URLs carry a `/folder/v-{version}/`
  cache-buster that isn't part of any key, so serving `.folder` straight
  from S3 would need a Bunny rewrite rule. Current assumption: `.folder`
  stays served through the app. Revisit only if origin load makes it worth
  it — dropping the cache-buster from URLs would be the rule-free option,
  at the cost of CDN purges on every file change.
- **Keep Blot-internal bookkeeping out of the public `folder/{blogID}/`
  key space** (§2.2.1) — worth an explicit check during Phase 0/1 that
  nothing beyond actual sync-client-written content ends up there.
- **`.list()`'s ordering guarantee** (§2.4) — sorted/deterministic vs.
  whatever order unbuffered iteration gives you is a real tradeoff between
  memory safety and predictable output; needs a decision once it's clear
  whether any consumer (the `folder` template local, in particular) depends
  on ordering.
- **Cross-instance SSD cache invalidation message schema** (§2.6) — extending
  the existing sync-completion pub/sub broadcast to carry specific changed
  paths (not just "this blog changed") is new wire-format surface between
  app instances; worth designing deliberately rather than bolting on ad hoc.
- **Reconciliation sweep frequency** for the SSD cache's ETag safety net
  (§2.6) — how often to spot-check cached entries against S3, analogous to
  the quota-reconciliation cadence question in §3.

---

## 6. Bottom line

Nothing in the current codebase blocks this. The two things that made me
initially skeptical about the "no performance penalty" goal — request-time
disk reads and cache-ability — are already solved or half-solved in the
existing code (Redis-cached rendered output, async build-time asset
generation, an existing SSD-LRU eviction pattern in the macserver client).
The abstraction layer itself is a big but mechanical refactor (~30-40 call
sites, no exotic IO patterns). The Bunny piece needs no changes at all for
the core migration, since Bunny already talks only to the Blot app server —
serving `.assets` straight from S3 is an optional later step (its keys
already match its public URLs, so no rewriting — §4.4), not a blocker. The remaining real design work,
beyond the refactor, is: (1) moving the per-blog sync lock off local disk
before multi-instance ever becomes real, and (2) rewriting `folder.js`'s
live directory listing to use paginated `.list()` calls rather than a
buffering `fs.readdir`. Best-case scenario worth actually pursuing, per
§4.9: the SSD caching layer itself — the single largest remaining piece of
new operational complexity in this whole plan (eviction, cross-instance
invalidation, reconciliation) — may turn out to be unnecessary, and that's
answerable by measurement before committing to build it, not something to
guess at upfront.

Suggested first concrete step, **once PR #1896 has landed** (the one hard
sequencing dependency, per the scope note at the top): build
`app/storage/index.js` as a pure same-behavior wrapper over local disk
(Phase 0 above) and migrate the generated-assets call sites onto it. That's
useful on its own, de-risks everything downstream, and gives you a real
place to hang quota accounting
and S3 dual-writes incrementally.
